---
scope: src/service/brainContract
description: "The single published source of the brain config's required-section contract, naming each section's marker, purpose, and role as a live runtime section or a seed written into the brain."
intent: >
  brainContract is the one place the brain config's required sections are
  defined. Each section carries its marker name, a one-line purpose, and a
  role: live sections are read at runtime, seed sections are written into
  the brain at setup, together with the brain location they land in. The
  parser, template seeding, repair, upgrade, and the agents all derive the
  section list and marker text from here instead of restating it. Adding a
  section here is the whole change: every config that lacks it reports
  invalid and routes the user to /aide:brain config.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - The parser, template seeding, repair, upgrade, and the agents all derive the required-section list and marker text from this one source. Changing a section here changes it for every consumer at once.
    - Every required section names its marker, a one-line purpose, and its role as live or seed, and every seed section also names the brain location it is written to.
    - Adding a section here makes every existing brain config that lacks it report the brain-aide-invalid status and route the user to /aide:brain config, with no other module editing a list of its own.
    - A config written for a custom backend that satisfies the contract is valid. Nothing in the contract assumes a particular brain backend.
    - The order and roles of the sections here are exactly what the parser enforces.
  undesired:
    - The section list or marker text restated anywhere outside this module, whether in the parser, the template tooling, repair, upgrade, or agent prose. A second copy drifts silently.
    - A required section that is optional, defaulted, or quietly filled in when absent. Required means a config without it is invalid.
    - A purpose line written in one backend's vocabulary, so a user wiring a custom backend cannot tell what the section should hold.
    - Contract entries whose order or roles drift from what the parser enforces, so a config that satisfies the published contract still fails to parse, or the reverse.
---
